[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054382 :question/id #uuid "5fe35108-2fa8-563e-862e-ad27d4ed5334" :question/math-academy-id "e-12860" :question/problem "Find the value of $k$ that satisfies the equation $-\\dfrac{k}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{k}{2}) & =(-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $k$ is being divided by $2.$ To isolate $k$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n    \t\t\\dfrac{k}{2} &=-\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t2 \\cdot \\dfrac{k}{2} &=2 \\cdot \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right) \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{2k}{2} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{\\cancel{2}k}{\\cancel{2}} &= -\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\tk &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054388 :question/id #uuid "cbce1708-81ba-5d37-ae21-fc8d94ebbc2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12894" :question/problem "If $\\dfrac{1}{7}=\\dfrac{x}{2},$ then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $x$ is being divided by $2.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $2.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{x}{2} & =2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & =\\frac{2}{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054395 :question/id #uuid "caf5354e-e240-5515-94c7-6c3caa2063af" :question/math-academy-id "e-12920" :question/problem "Find the value of $x - \\dfrac{3}{2}y$ when $x=2$ and $y=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Replacing $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $-2$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{3}{2}y &= 2-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ &= 2+(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-2) \\\\ &= 2+3 \\\\ &= 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054398 :question/id #uuid "21fccd11-6107-53b8-beb0-d0c39f23bc17" :question/math-academy-id "e-12923" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\left( 5 \\div (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5÷(-2)^{2} &= 5÷4 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overbrace{5÷(-2)^{2}}^{\\frac{5}{4}})\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) &= \\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\ &= -\\frac{5}{4}\\times \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ &= -\\frac{5\\times 4}{4\\times 5} \\\\ &= -\\frac{\\cancel{5}\\times \\cancel{4}}{\\cancel{4}\\times \\cancel{5}} \\\\ &= -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054401 :question/id #uuid "82a9a75f-ab0e-54c3-9a0d-82c8497714cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-12958" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-\\dfrac{y}{8} \\leq -4.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $8.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-8.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{8}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-4 \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-8)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ (-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge 32 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}8)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{8}) & \\ge 32 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{y}{8} & \\ge 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $8$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot \\frac{y}{8} & \\ge 32 \\\\ \\frac{8y}{8} & \\ge 32 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{8}y}{\\cancel{8}} & \\ge 32 \\\\ y & \\ge 32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y \\geq 32.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054513 :question/id #uuid "27b89a42-7f31-579b-af21-b33ebe36f749" :question/math-academy-id "e-31" :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Determine $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\left(\\dfrac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2}\\right)^3.$\n\n![](images/50/5059a2cee34ad8547bf1af6239ab4a8356e715cd920707cf5f33d2de105a5c1d.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3.$ Therefore, using the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}{(\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2-7x}{xf(x)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2-7(2)}{(2\\cdot 3)+2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-12}{8})}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-3}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054519 :question/id #uuid "3d6b32ee-3385-5fba-91a5-66c0bbb13dea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3565" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2}\\left(\\dfrac{f(x)}{x^2-1}\\right)^{2/3}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/58/583e67513466b35aafcae69c81d5703b45ffada4bdc08bb34de15ee6ce1d53e5.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the power rule and the quotient rules,\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}{(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1})}^{2/3} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)})}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that the limit of the numerator is\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2} f(x)=1.\n$$\n\n We also calculate the limit of the denominator as\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1) & =(-2)^{2}-1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n So we get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}{(\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1})}^{2/3} & ={(\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)}{{lim}_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}-1)})}^{2/3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054520 :question/id #uuid "578a205e-7879-5f8e-b7b5-a91a134ec5b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-369" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054542 :question/id #uuid "de20802c-6a7e-5d77-b2ce-26f98dc68b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4095" :question/problem "Express $3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4)$ as a single logarithm." :question/worked-solution "Using the power rule for logarithms, we can rewrite the first term of the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{3})+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we can combine the first two terms to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the quotient rule for logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(8x)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{8x}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3\\log(2)+\\log(x)-\\log(4) = \\log\\left(2x\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054580 :question/id #uuid "2a156814-63e0-5686-ac89-2859dbf0d958" :question/math-academy-id "e-76" :question/problem "$\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\pi/4}\\dfrac{\\sin^2{x}-\\cos^2{x}}{2\\sin{x}\\cos{2x}}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → π/4)\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= (\\sin^{2} (\\frac{π}{4}) - \\cos^{2} (\\frac{π}{4}))/(2\\sin (\\frac{π}{4})\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= (1 - 1)/(\\sqrt{2}\\cos (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. The double angle formula for cosine will be useful in this case:\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x}=\\cos^2{x}-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the double angle formula above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → π/4)\\frac{\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x}{2\\sin x\\cos 2x} &= lim_(x → π/4)(\\sin^{2} x - \\cos^{2} x)/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → π/4)(- (\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x))/(2\\sin x(\\cos^{2} x - \\sin^{2} x)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → π/4)(-\\frac{1}{2\\sin x}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054584 :question/id #uuid "b9498461-7676-5414-980e-d9c4bd610aaf" :question/math-academy-id "e-77" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{1-\\cos x}{3\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} (\\frac{x}{2})) &= (1 - \\cos (0))/(3\\sin^{2} (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 1}{3 \\cdot 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. Again we will use the double angle formula for cosine, but in a different form:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}=1-2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0)(1 - \\cos x)/(3\\sin^{2} (\\frac{x}{2})) &= lim_(x → 0)(1 - (1 - 2\\sin^{2} (\\frac{x}{2})))/(3\\sin^{2} (\\frac{x}{2})) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)(2\\sin^{2} (\\frac{x}{2}))/(3\\sin^{2} (\\frac{x}{2})) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 0)\\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054585 :question/id #uuid "988c2bee-9602-5182-bf19-6c7dfbeba308" :question/math-academy-id "e-78" :question/problem "$\\lim_\\limits{x\\to (-\\pi/2)}\\dfrac{\\cos^2x}{1+\\sin x}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= (\\cos^{2} (-\\frac{π}{2}))/((1 + \\sin (-\\frac{π}{2}))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need to simplify the expression. In particular, we will use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2{x}=1-\\sin^2{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} &= lim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{\\cos^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (- π/2))\\frac{1 - \\sin^{2} x}{1 + \\sin x} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (- π/2))((1 + \\sin x)(1 - \\sin x))/(1 + \\sin x) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (- π/2))(1 - \\sin x) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\sin (-\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 1 - (-1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4979b44-85eb-5d96-81dd-44db6df749da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3} (x) + \\log_{3} (y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051255} {:answer/id #uuid "d1154f68-ae89-5073-bf5b-b7906b1b674c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\log_{3} (x) - \\log_{3} (y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051256} {:answer/id #uuid "f4423cf5-3dde-5f58-9da4-d8c7e76729b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3} (x - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051257} {:answer/id #uuid "0231cebb-8746-5479-8882-d20c4e95471d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_{3} (x) - \\log_{3} (y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051258} {:answer/id #uuid "25615b77-93a4-57f4-835d-e9389e92f131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\log_{3} (x - y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051259}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3c77f0a1-7c51-5e4a-ac9a-a980d9211f43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051254}] :question/id #uuid "b47500fa-8b56-5c05-945b-0311701f0dfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-1100" :question/problem "What is $\\log_{3} (\\frac{x^{2}}{y})$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b56a6dea-6c8e-5fa5-9b5b-9c3a0d2ca34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log (x) + 3\\log (y) - \\log (z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051273} {:answer/id #uuid "be3f403a-db0e-5f33-8101-a0925b25258f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\log (x) + \\log (y) - \\log (z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051274} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d5a02c-9e66-5ae1-b9be-d269e089a9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\log (x) - \\log (y) - \\log (z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051275} {:answer/id #uuid "2c06cb6a-8f01-5845-8098-a6cd1b8d9d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log (x) - 3\\log (z) - \\log (y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051276} {:answer/id #uuid "69cc2564-aa09-5975-bf27-8509d896ed40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log (x) + \\log (z) - 3\\log (y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051277}] :answer-fiel