{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330909} {:answer/id #uuid "7f492b9d-6afa-598a-9228-2c305e5229d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331073} {:answer/id #uuid "b3abbbd4-f00c-5565-9f4d-d1ae4e3cf803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331535} {:answer/id #uuid "f29131c4-16c3-54d0-8eb6-c764e2d48fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3abbbd4-f00c-5565-9f4d-d1ae4e3cf803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331535} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e69b9a7-aeba-5f73-aa1e-59bfa9d09e08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "421782a5-f8ee-574b-bd35-7a68ca94b6ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-53746" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y-2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ (2x+y)+(x-2y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2x+y}{x-2y})}_{(0,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 0+1}{0-2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0+1}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b2fe475-6257-5ccc-8db9-274c6fa55fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534447} {:answer/id #uuid "72d91a6d-fd5d-5373-9a45-1598ad4a0d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534508} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9d26f0-861d-5a0a-95be-6706bd54fd00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534528} {:answer/id #uuid "95f4279b-1934-5ce0-b76b-a852503dc885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534594} {:answer/id #uuid "f104ac01-a346-5997-bc8f-5574e571e0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b2fe475-6257-5ccc-8db9-274c6fa55fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534447} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7b8292-2cc9-59fa-8427-59bb85b1cab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58adfdfb-8a6a-5b73-a9ed-33d397b103fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64339" :question/problem "![](images/19/198f3a8a98e9650cf53c81a24a4fc96e08a743bdd6c48e47eb4b59f5bc8e4f2c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is the measure of the angle $\\theta ?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](images/5b/5b8cfbcd58092a40f34ed969e9772981615db54eb5c20c5b7543e038a6e0fade.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta $ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96517480-c58a-580a-bd6c-7724f8c25364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054010} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f3604e-93c4-543c-85d4-015c8fe7e1ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054011} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d3f4da-921c-581d-b2f2-9c94396b9176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054012} {:answer/id #uuid "1bd17a35-4d96-574e-94c2-099f33db7738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054013} {:answer/id #uuid "2d69af09-12b4-5f14-a76b-d95e41ad99a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d69af09-12b4-5f14-a76b-d95e41ad99a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054014} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c9c30ac-745b-5ac6-8f1f-5c84b0f7083a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4f6e3a6-36a7-5dcc-b176-d20ea50c924d" :question/math-academy-id "q-66882" :question/problem "${log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}⁡X=\\frac{{log}_{b}⁡X}{{log}_{b}⁡a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=36$ and $X=\\sqrt[3]{6}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $6,$ we set $b=6.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6}) & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡(\\sqrt[3]{6})}{{log}_{6}⁡(36)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{1/3})}{{log}_{6}⁡({6}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbf1f72-06b0-520d-bcce-fe7a87516e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054028} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed13fb1-485e-541f-8244-edff58a9edcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054029} {:answer/id #uuid "402ab3f3-62da-5651-83a1-94331abbd494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054030} {:answer/id #uuid "5d896cad-d52b-57a0-9b03-cd6b8039620e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054031} {:answer/id #uuid "601b00f5-8338-5dfb-9271-e19b674aab25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d896cad-d52b-57a0-9b03-cd6b8039620e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054031} :answer-field/id #uuid "adf83468-7688-50ec-b222-9820046725f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a406bc65-b6be-5e0c-9457-9bc53f06a1e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-68933" :question/problem "Evaluate $(-\\frac{1}{3})÷((-4)^{3}÷8).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, consider the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3}÷8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{3}÷8 &= (-64)÷8 \\\\ &= \\frac{(-64)}{8} \\\\ &= -8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{3})÷((-4)^{3}÷8) &= (-\\frac{1}{3})÷(-8) \\\\ &= (-\\frac{1}{3})\\times (-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ &= \\frac{1\\times 1}{3\\times 8} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059442 :question/id #uuid "2385b6c3-1f86-59fe-96bf-d42ed5cd1b56" :question/math-academy-id "e-139" :question/problem "If $y=-\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x}} = -\\dfrac{3}{x^{1/2}} = -3x^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-3x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{-1/2}) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}x^{-3/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059475 :question/id #uuid "a218df38-fa61-5ffd-b1bd-77422b946d4c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3149" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression\n$\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1}$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta} {\\cos \\theta + 1} = \\left(\\cot \\theta + \\csc \\theta\\right)\\div \\left(\\cos \\theta + 1\\right)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n\\cot \\theta = \\dfrac {\\cos \\theta} {\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nand we substitute them into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\cot θ + \\csc θ) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) &= (\\frac{\\cos θ}{\\sin θ} + \\frac{1}{\\sin θ}) \\div (\\cos θ + 1) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{\\cos θ + 1}{\\sin θ}) \\div (\\cos θ + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\div  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta }} ) \\cdot  ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cos   \\theta  + 1}{\\sin   \\theta  ( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}{\\sin   \\theta  \\cancel{( \\cos   \\theta  + 1 )}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sin   \\theta }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\csc   \\theta \\end{array}}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059483 :question/id #uuid "84630f24-0d62-5ab3-8f9d-e03e815820cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-3288" :question/problem "Consider the following triangle:\n\n![](images/e0/e03942073ddfd8672fe5c5e4bd7bc81f0dc22f8682e3a364b001c280b315e721.png)\n\nCalculate the measure of the angle $\\angle A$ to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use the Law of Cosines to solve for angle $A$:\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\frac{b^{2} + c^{2} - a^{2}}{2bc}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\na=9,\\quad b=7,\\quad c=12\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos A &= (7^{2} + 12^{2} - 9^{2})/(2(7)(12)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{49 + 144 - 81}{168} \\\\\n&= \\frac{112}{168} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= \\arccos (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\\n&= 48.19^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059487 :question/id #uuid "056204bc-53b9-5a32-a53c-0f728729fd9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3332" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right) = 3\\cos^2 x \\sec^2 x - 3\\cos^2 x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{cos}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡x}}-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity $\\sin^2 x = 1-\\cos^2 x$ to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, as follows:\n\n$$\n3(1-\\cos^2 x) = 3\\cdot \\sin^2 x = 3\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\cos^2 x \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right)= 3\\sin^2 x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059493 :question/id #uuid "4cd24c10-1838-5f4b-ac88-cfbfc2ec4c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-3518" :question/problem "Express $\\sin\\left(x - 30^{\\circ} \\right )$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u\\cos v - \\cos u\\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=30^{\\circ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059494 :question/id #uuid "7341638b-3c0f-5c10-8b7d-a2c313607bed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3519" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)$ without using a calculator.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(x-y) = \\sin x\\cos y - \\cos x\\sin y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059495 :question/id #uuid "652143ae-b765-5abe-a7e9-a90ec15dd38f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3521" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\tan \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$.*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(x-y) = \\dfrac{\\tan{x}-\\tan{y}}{1+\\tan{x}\\tan{y}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (\\frac{π}{12}) &= \\tan (\\frac{π}{3} - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= (\\tan (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\tan (\\frac{π}{4}))/(1 + \\tan (\\frac{π}{3})\\tan (\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{1 + \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (\\frac{π}{12}) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} \\\\\n&= ((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 2\\sqrt{3} + (-1)^{2})/((\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 1^{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3} + 1}{3 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059496 :question/id #uuid "1d8679d3-8922-5471-811f-60c8c41c853a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3522" :question/problem "Express $\\cos\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059497 :question/id #uuid "bdb79801-7c8f-5b39-8730-85dcc3d9cdd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3523" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\cos \\left( -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x - y) = \\cos{x}\\cos{y} + \\sin{x}\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059498 :question/id #uuid "6fdd424e-5136-54b5-a2ef-c4d658597374" :question/math-academy-id "e-3525" :question/problem "Write $\\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ as an expression containing $\\tan{x}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(u-v) = \\dfrac{\\tan{u}-\\tan{v}}{1+\\tan{u}\\tan{v}}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for tangent with $u=x$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (x - \\frac{π}{4}) &= (\\tan x - \\tan (\\frac{π}{4}))/(1 + \\tan x\\tan (\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{1 + \\tan x \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\tan x - 1}{\\tan x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059500 :question/id #uuid "35535b08-b3d8-5037-a821-6815577169d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3618" :question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin{2x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right) .\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{24})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{24}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059502 :question/id #uuid "539b5b86-7b31-52c9-be04-e80d6799b68a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3623" :question/problem "Without using a calculator, find the exact value of $3\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\sin{x}\\cos{x} = \\sin 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\dfrac {\\pi}{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) \\cos \\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} {8} \\right) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above by $\\dfrac 1 2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\, {\\color{black}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)}\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\right)} } = 3 \\left( \\color{black} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :question/id #uuid "b092bc79-d369-5ff0-a6bb-5fc059699275" :question/math-academy-id "e-3654" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for $y = 3 x^4 + 5x+ \\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =3(4){x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3){x}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059508 :question/id #uuid "2b66406d-a2c6-5fd0-bc19-ae80d8efbbdd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3770" :question/problem "Express $\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 5\\right)}$ using the corresponding cofunction." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the cofunction of $\\cos x$ (**co**sine) is $\\sin x$ (sine). The corresponding identity is given by\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - x \\right)\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\ninto the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos(\\frac{π}{5}) &= \\sin(\\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{π}{5}) \\\\\n&= \\sin (\\frac{3π}{10})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 5\\right)} = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac {3 \\pi} {10}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059529 :question/id #uuid "c5d69bcb-d11f-567a-84b3-63935ae813b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4300" :question/problem "Find the angle that the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2, 3 \\rangle$ makes with the positive $x$-axis. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "We want the angle $\\theta$, as shown below. Note that the vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/50/501f85a2a6d6d69e737cd507c542869b12494c3ce4979212e806ad223f89447a.png)\n\nApplying the formula for the angle that a vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan θ &= \\frac{a_{y}}{a_{x}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{-2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\arctan (-\\frac{3}{2}) + n \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 56.3^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nIn this case, our vector is located in the $2$nd quadrant, so we want a solution $90^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$.\n\nTo get a solution in the desired range, we choose $n=1$ and get\n\n$$\nθ \\mid \\approx - 56.3^{∘} + 180^{∘}; = 123.7^{∘}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059531 :question/id #uuid "92de462e-377b-5fa9-b858-f2ccb7bc7f70" :question/math-academy-id "e-4303" :question/problem "Find the smallest angle that the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2, -1 \\rangle$ makes with the **positive $y$-axis**. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b3d4d12313dcbb5d882ce547af97037694ff6e12fd134c7f9fd1243ac2bdc1e.png)\n\nFirst, we need to find the angle $\\theta$ that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the positive $x$-axis. Applying the usual formula, we obtain the equation\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{a_y}{a_x} = \\dfrac{-1}{2} = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThe solutions of this equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\arctan (-\\frac{1}{2}) + n \\cdot 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 26.6^{∘} + n \\cdot 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer.\n\nIn this case, our vector is located in the $4$th quadrant. Choosing $n=0$, we get\n\n$$\nθ \\mid \\approx - 26.6^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the angle $\\theta'$ that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes with the positive $y$-axis, we add $90^\\circ$ to the absolute value of the angle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^{′} &= \\mid θ \\mid + 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 26.6^{∘} + 90^{∘} \\\\\n&= 116.6^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059534 :question/id #uuid "b90c0085-4e6d-5ec2-a463-4aaf951c040a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4307" :question/problem "Find the components of $\\mathbf{a}$ if it makes an angle $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ with the *positive $y$-axis* and $\\mid \\mathbf{a}|=60$." :question/worked-solution "Note that our vector lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\nFirst, to find the angle that out vector makes with the positive $x$-axis, we add\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nUsing our formulas, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{x} &= \\mid a\\begin{vmatrix}\\cos θ \\\\ = 60\\cos (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\ = 60 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ =-30\\sqrt{3} \\\\ a_{y}|=\\end{vmatrix}a \\mid \\sin θ \\\\\n&= 60\\sin (\\frac{5π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a} &=\\langle a_x, a_y \\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle -30\\sqrt{3}, 30 \\rangle \\\\\n&={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 30 \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 30\\end{array} \\right]}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059540 :question/id #uuid "7bf2ce5d-bc04-5bc8-8183-13d6e60d972b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4319" :question/problem "Suppose that the vectors $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ are two vectors defined in the $x$-$y$ plane. The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ makes a counterclockwise angle of $127^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and has $\\mid \\mathbf{a}|=4$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ makes a clockwise angle of $23^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and has $\\mid \\mathbf{b}|=3$. Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{a}$ makes an angle of $127^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis and $\\mathbf{b}$ makes an angle of $-23^\\circ$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= \\mid 127^{∘} - (-23^{∘})|= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/e1/e16c58888810038f7ef5b5794e74d87fe8af6b273f0d527be94181cb79f5477f.png)\n\nNow, using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\cos 150^{∘} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\\n&=-6\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059542 :question/id #uuid "9a59379a-c212-50a5-90dd-481783e1ea5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4321" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ be two vectors with magnitudes $\\mid \\mathbf{a}|=13\\sqrt{17}$ and $\\mid \\mathbf{b}|=146$, and let the angle between the vectors be $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Since the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nthe vectors are perpendicular and thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0\n$$\n\nWe can confirm this. Using the formula for the dot product, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}a & \\cdot & b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos θ \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= 13\\sqrt{17} \\cdot 146 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059627 :question/id #uuid "0ce71d86-7470-5d53-ac94-20b473889dee" :question/math-academy-id "e-9983" :question/problem "Express $\\sin\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin{u}\\cos{v} + \\cos{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83268552-78e0-5908-b0ea-2ecf96f1c967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2\\sin (\\frac{x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056437} {:answer/id #uuid "d606826e-c9e5-55b3-b28b-4071d1576fa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sin (\\frac{x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056438} {:answer/id #uuid "05fa6e72-0418-530f-99c9-a88262cc0684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2\\sin (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056439} {:answer/id #uuid "3065d8d0-766b-572c-ba8b-b8dcf3