 #uuid "a7570d1a-a618-5ed6-9675-a5192d884029" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c67cd7a-c282-58e6-83e7-15a2075597f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-65154" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows the unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the standard position?" :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to $-{45}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/a3/a3106bc6788215147adfed66ac038523a73191a9b0182f35089bd1d7b2f87787.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dd9be9c-02b5-5c7a-8757-9270ae3383f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333850} {:answer/id #uuid "7da16ec7-555e-5e35-b3d5-985ecf58d11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334110} {:answer/id #uuid "bb28a6ac-fcb8-5d1c-ba07-27c9ce977bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334621} {:answer/id #uuid "cb85f976-13dc-5c1c-90ad-38f6af5380bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334762} {:answer/id #uuid "eda967d8-610a-5009-b3ee-4cadd13cad53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb28a6ac-fcb8-5d1c-ba07-27c9ce977bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334621} :answer-field/id #uuid "13856fdf-975c-52a5-b9af-7544e9333277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86112d28-25a2-5d86-9c7b-fc0c2d1692c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-65438" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={45}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{45}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99acfb95-1188-5592-a613-4ca82391ae75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sin (x - 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058795} {:answer/id #uuid "bc63f627-87dc-5ea3-9fba-b387da1b0d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sin (5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058796} {:answer/id #uuid "13afea39-2bbe-523b-9082-5ed53ab9da33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sin (\\frac{x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058797} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9a76a0-9165-5fbb-af2d-fcf20ed861b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sin (x) + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058798} {:answer/id #uuid "734e501d-eb35-5bae-9d05-755f7e761478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5\\sin (x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058799}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ac770323-8828-5667-a48e-47b1629b3a57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058794}] :question/id #uuid "868c8ec2-f438-5fe6-b82b-d269d5684fa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-6574" :question/problem "![](images/55/550ca401fc326aab97e9c825960196e0b12b6a42ca36dfa15ec57cc77ee79433.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dddef715-15d1-5688-9498-02a18f5a82ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058849} {:answer/id #uuid "1bbfc72d-60dc-598a-9e50-9f1f78d315f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058850} {:answer/id #uuid "2b4db2de-8f65-5588-b118-1aba6c4f87a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058851} {:answer/id #uuid "30236ad8-46b9-5497-9506-c0cfd32bcde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058852} {:answer/id #uuid "785d1c3c-d3a1-5646-8df8-6839743174cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "785d1c3c-d3a1-5646-8df8-6839743174cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058853} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5489cf7-7cba-528f-8bd9-9e7c58d401ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eddd7f11-274f-563c-bc35-743113376d12" :question/math-academy-id "q-67325" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{1}{2x}=\\frac{3}{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero.\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2x}{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nThe above equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n-2x=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{2})}\\cdot (\\cancel{-2}x) & =-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c71a3a11-3eba-5020-9597-3de35a5ab785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058867} {:answer/id #uuid "c768ab08-49e9-5e5c-928d-98edae762f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058868} {:answer/id #uuid "cf329fcc-fb6f-59d1-adcc-57bc40719b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058869} {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} {:answer/id #uuid "2c81b1e5-f63c-5724-81ac-8400764b9ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22218b3e-4939-5119-bb7f-f0028c856a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058870} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfde7113-aa52-5603-817f-a4c288821339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b4e7858-1264-5def-86e5-7598a8adf5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-67329" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{x-3}=\\frac{2}{x+6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=-6$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{x+6}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $6.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{6}=\\frac{3x+18}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot \\frac{x-3}{6} & =6\\cdot \\frac{3x+18}{6} \\\\ x-3 & =3x+18 \\\\ x-3x & =18+3 \\\\ -2x & =21 \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})(-2x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ (-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{2}})(-\\cancel{2}x) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})21 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe93af-c815-5f2c-ac75-9a584d561ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058879} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d50060-ed5a-55ba-98a5-e136d622f6bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d7edad-f24a-5a39-b3a9-bdf0e222d37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058881} {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} {:answer/id #uuid "4335e387-f8cb-50d1-a3a5-f1a739c9876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3af8e372-8b94-5bca-966e-b13590d4c251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058882} :answer-field/id #uuid "084ec80c-c0ed-58a4-b621-614f03d00659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09320d7a-d867-531b-9740-66504912149d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67332" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{3x+2}=-\\frac{4}{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=0$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x+2}{2}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6x+4}{4}=-\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{6x+4}{4} & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{4}) \\\\ 6x+4 & =-x \\\\ 6x+x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89218747-18b1-5304-8caa-dd59a13d395d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4})^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058897} {:answer/id #uuid "9853513d-449c-54b9-bee0-da0022fa27da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4})^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058898} {:answer/id #uuid "da30573d-0599-5e7e-b33b-32777031b4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2})^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058899} {:answer/id #uuid "2d203dc7-7fca-5bb8-9be7-f5fb1b27a0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2})^{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058900} {:answer/id #uuid "7323d09f-ed70-555b-b264-b1b2f56ee0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2})^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058901}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7bd5116-e184-5e11-b64b-19f5ea6b925f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058896}] :question/id #uuid "678be690-dbdd-53c9-ba17-1fa622e9f7f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-68079" :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{1}{2})^{3} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{3}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b53dbc7-9835-56e7-b765-d464d3b4e31e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2})^{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058921} {:answer/id #uuid "a515dd32-c50d-5fc5-a8a5-72d546382c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16})^{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058922} {:answer/id #uuid "ad85558b-370b-5b10-a182-8f58084343b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{16})^{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058923} {:answer/id #uuid "3032ccde-fae2-5456-a6cb-848d4268a6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4})^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058924} {:answer/id #uuid "34e67e86-c21b-5346-9eaa-9f4bc77038f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4})^{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058925}] :answer-field/id #uuid "278f5cfd-90dc-5b01-ba3b-8afd64680526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058920}] :question/id #uuid "544b38f7-745b-57d8-bc3d-22266cc44488" :question/math-academy-id "q-68299" :question/problem "What is $(-\\frac{1}{4})^{9} \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4})^{4}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ade3d95-57eb-5fc4-b8e6-e17185c28e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059233} {:answer/id #uuid "90b69a8d-95a7-5e71-8185-46e77ff1a259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059234} {:answer/id #uuid "0f0fea1b-6359-5c56-baa8-d40fd31f715e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059235} {:answer/id #uuid "4706c649-48b5-5e11-a7c2-2b4e4663b034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059236} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2ab9c7-f4e5-575a-be22-d0e9cad916bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059237}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1a645e8e-11bb-531d-baa7-0dc160e83dc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059232}] :question/id #uuid "7ae0fadf-5abc-58e3-9beb-87c60965199b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9083" :question/problem "What is $\\arccos (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063933 :question/id #uuid "7f0eeea0-e493-5287-9633-8128758d5d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-125" :question/problem "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=3x^2$ at the point $x=5$ using the definition." :question/worked-solution "Let's use the definition\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nIn our problem, we have $a=5$, and so by the definition we have\n\n$$\nf^{′}(5) = lim_(h → 0)(f(5 + h) - f(5))/(h)\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(5) &= lim_(h → 0)((3(5 + h)^{2} - 3(5)^{2})/(h)) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{75 + 30h + 3h^{2} - 75}{h}) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{30h + 3h^{2}}{h}) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(30 + 3h) \\\\\n&= 30 + 3(0) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063934 :question/id #uuid "95edcdc3-a675-5963-9e1b-e9eb0595327d" :question/math-academy-id "e-126" :question/problem "Using the limit of a difference quotient, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=2x-x^2$ at the point $x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is equal to $f'(2)$.\n\nLet's use the equivalent definition for $f'(a)$, given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nUsing the above definition with $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(2)} {x-2}\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(x → 2)(([2x - x^{2}] - [2(2) - (2)^{2}])/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(\\frac{-x^{2} + 2x}{x - 2}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)((-x(x - 2))/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(-x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-2$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063947 :question/id #uuid "78675372-1e29-5088-8907-52d9e54124e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13083" :question/problem "What is $\\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 4\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 7\\right)}$ expressed as a single fraction?" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division.  So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{7}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{7}{3}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7}) &= \\frac{1}{4}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3}) \\\\ &= -\\frac{1\\times 7}{4\\times 3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a positive number times a negative number gives a negative number."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063948 :question/id #uuid "3bad12f4-14b2-5ab6-a1db-9d460b9a26ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-13084" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-3)}{(-\\frac{3}{5})}=-3÷(-\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $-3 =- \\dfrac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{5}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{5}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) &= (-\\frac{3}{1})÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\ &= (-\\frac{3}{1})\\times (-\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\ &= \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} \\\\ &= \\frac{15}{3} \\\\ &= 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)} = 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a negative number times a negative number gives a positive number."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063949 :question/id #uuid "87974d27-c494-5b1a-84e9-03e39f3e0b1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13086" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{3}{2})÷6 &= (-\\frac{3}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ &= -\\frac{3\\times 1}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n- \\dfrac{3}{12}  = - \\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3}  = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n \n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2})}{6} &= \\frac{-3}{2\\times 6} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{12} \\\\ &= -\\frac{3÷3}{12÷3} \\\\ &= -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063959 :question/id #uuid "347baa8c-4ba0-559a-a5b6-81e024054af6" :question/math-academy-id "e-13265" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{3}{7} - \\left( -\\dfrac{11}{7} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To subtract $-\\dfrac{11}{7}$ from $\\dfrac{3}{7},$ we combine the two negative signs to make a single plus sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}-(-\\frac{11}{7}) & =\\frac{3}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a common denominator of $7,$ so we combine numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{7}+\\frac{11}{7}=\\frac{3+11}{7}=\\frac{14}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can simplify the resulting fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{7} & =14÷7=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If two fractions have different denominators, we need to put them over a common denominator first and *then* add or subtract!"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063962 :question/id #uuid "2fb03f0f-25c2-57a6-9e33-da837af2f339" :question/math-academy-id "e-1329" :question/problem "Factor the expression $y^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}} x + a^2 =  (x+{\\color{blue}{a}})^2 .\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\ny^2 + \\dfrac{2}{5}y + \\dfrac{1}{25}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with ${\\color{blue}{a}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063968 :question/id #uuid "62a47fad-0c27-5e6d-a21f-8b298ac11159" :question/math-academy-id "e-1355" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4x^2}{y^2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $4, x^2,$ and $y^2$ are all perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot x\\cdot 2\\cdot x}{y\\cdot y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2x}{y}\\cdot \\frac{2x}{y}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2x}{y})}^{2}} & =|\\frac{2x}{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|2x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2|\\cdot |x|}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2|x|}{|y|}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063986 :question/id #uuid "0317d27d-9501-5de5-a55a-b9a73ce39500" :question/math-academy-id "e-14306" :question/problem "Simplify $-6x^2\\left(\\dfrac{x^3}{2}+3x^2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{x}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+3{x}^{2}) &= (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ &= (-6{x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3})+(-6{x}^{2})(3{x}^{2}) \\\\ &= ((-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})({x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})+((-6)\\cdot 3)({x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}) \\\\ &= (-3)({x}^{3+2})+(-18)({x}^{2+2}) \\\\ &= -3{x}^{5}-18{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We provide the answer in *standard form*, with the terms arranged by degree in descending order."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063999 :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064011 :question/id #uuid "0f8652d9-5d83-5d2c-93ae-2f3a4fc4e3e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-204" :question/problem "If $y= x \\arccos x,$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and using the formula for the derivative of $\\arccos x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x) & =x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x)+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064018 :question/id #uuid "6264adc4-c5ab-5b84-b89f-27b4382baa0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-220" :question/problem "![](images/26/2677121139896da73eca5277c7c23f193467001eabba976cb48b2232f4bcbad6.png)\n\nIn a testing facility, a scientist is increasing the diagonals of a simple square at a rate of $12 \\text{ mm}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate, in squared millimeters per minute, is the area of the square increasing when the length of the diagonal is $6\\, \\text{mm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $12\\,\\textrm{mm/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=6.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =12\\cdot 6=72{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064019 :question/id #uuid "7187c402-9949-5671-af33-8929bc5344a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-221" :question/problem "The sides of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to twice the rate of increase of the diagonal. What is the area of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation and the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t}$, so replacing $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm dt}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =h\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} & =h\\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 2 & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at that particular moment, we have $h=2,$ and therefore the area of the square at that moment is\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{2^2}{2} = 2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064024 :question/id #uuid "fba09533-20f9-5e4c-9786-cfbe7b179512" :question/math-academy-id "e-226" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to half the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the radius of the circle at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A = \\pi r^2,$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA =  \\pi r^2 =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{C}{2\\pi} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{C^2}{4\\pi}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C = 2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\pi  \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064029 :question/id #uuid "a669adb9-c12e-520d-994b-cddf4f9fedb6" :question/math-academy-id "e-233" :question/problem "Maria is flying a kite $K$ at an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to the horizontal. The kite's shadow $S$ is projected onto the ground vertically below the kite. If the kite is moving towards Maria with a speed of $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ what is the speed of the kite's shadow?\n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\n![](images/1a/1a00ce10db2d8a0fa292bd3ca67744e7cbaafcf1861cbbdc049832d287926d28.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69402f16f81a6653e1fdd313c314ac72db7f5514501af1ac147b5e499b305233.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between María and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between Maria and the kite.\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm dt} = -5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}$ (negative because the distance between Maria and the kite is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot (-5) = -2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $2.5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064030 :question/id #uuid "7c740ffe-3391-52cc-b8fd-7fb9a54e58cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-234" :question/problem "Yurii is flying a kite $K$ with a string of length $45\\,\\textrm{m}.$ The angle of elevation of the kite $\\theta$ decreases at a rate of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ radians per second. At what rate is the altitude of the kite is decreasing at the moment when $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}?$\n\n![](images/83/83f63ada35f2d9f64cd4b5013ccfcc86a97e48c7d19b49c403d21222a2e14d7b.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/04/04784c267488ef2073c526aed683e86ca0a00d00af64eb994ce596c0cce629d9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Yurii. The quantity $z=45$ meters does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{45} \\\\ y & =45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =45(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate that the kite is losing altitude is $\\dfrac{3\\pi\\sqrt3}{2} \\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064031 :question/id #uuid "808feb74-65a8-5339-b263-f92ef91bfefb" :question/math-academy-id "e-235" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{32}\\pi \\, \\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr}.$ The height of the cone is always a quarter of its radius. At what rate is the radius of the base decreasing when the radius is $3 \\,\\textrm{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{1}{32} \\,\\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}$ when $r = 3 \\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is a quarter of its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{4}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}r\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{12}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{32}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the base of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{72} \\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{hr}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064032 :question/id #uuid "6ce9d38b-08d9-5372-b501-298970a2f11f" :question/math-academy-id "e-236" :question/problem "A conical flask has a height of $20\\,\\text{cm}.$ The radius at the bottom of the flask is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ The flask has a very narrow opening at the top, and a chemist is pouring a chemical into the flask. If the level (i.e., height) of the chemical in the flask is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}$, find the rate at which volume of the chemical is increasing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/eb/eb454cec7cd4c38997ee5c8e9e29fed87f491f5f47066271613bb51e0aae5363.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the flask. We call $H=20\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical flask, and $R=10\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the chemical level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the flask to the surface of the chemical, $r$ the radius of the flask at the chemical surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the flask.  All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = -2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the flask to the chemical surface decreases as the chemist pours the chemical into the flask. \n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume being poured into the flask is changing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, we wish to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt}$ when the level of the chemical is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-4 \\\\  & =20-4 \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the chemical must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {(16)}^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-128\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of chemical is increasing at a rate of $128\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{min}.$"}]
